\section{Helmet constructions}\label{sec:S5}

\subsection{The fixed adult helmet and its placements}\label{sec:S5.1}

The centered placement puts each head's frame where the adult's lies in the sample recording (its device-to-head transform), keeping the ear line in place. The primary placement, top contact, raises the head from there until the nearest magnetometer coil center is << F.g3b_cfg_clearance >> mm from the scalp, the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing plus a margin. This best-fit assumption, not documented practice, favors Neuromag. A child in an adult helmet may be placed centrally and repositioned toward the expected source \citep{gaetz2008}, and a small child's shoulders can stop the head short of the top \citep{tesan2010}. The other placements bound this choice.

A family of << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements, chosen from the geometry alone, tests the sensitivity to placement. It comprises top and back contact, shifts of ±<< F.g3b_cfg_translation >> mm left--right and front--back, and rotations of ±<< F.g3b_cfg_pitch >>° pitch, ±<< F.g3b_cfg_roll >>° roll and ±<< F.g3b_cfg_yaw >>° yaw, each raised to contact. One lateral shift was infeasible for child C, leaving << F.g3b_child_c_n_family_feasible >>. Three further placements are the centered pose, the laterally centered pose (moved to the helmet's midline) raised to contact, and true contact at the Dewar spacing.

The adult's own $D$ was << F.g3b_adult_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB at its measured pose, << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB at top contact, << F.g3b_adult_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB at laterally centered contact and << F.g3b_adult_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB at true contact. Over the family it spanned << F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB (<< F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> dB), top contact ranking << F.g3b_adult_top_rank_in_family >> from the lowest. Against the adult placed by the same rule, the principal smaller heads' $\Delta$ was << F.g3b_principal_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at the centered pose and << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at top contact. It was << F.g3b_principal_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at laterally centered contact and << F.g3b_principal_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at true contact. In the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}, $\Delta$ was << F.g3b_provisional_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>, << F.g3b_provisional_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>, << F.g3b_provisional_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> and << F.g3b_provisional_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at the same four placements.

With sensor plus brain noise, the principal smaller heads' point estimates of $\Delta$ at top contact kept their sign in the other comparisons. They were << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_grad_ib_delta_range >> dB for the dense array against the gradiometers, << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_mag_ib_delta_range >> dB against the magnetometers and << F.g3b_principal_matched_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB for the site-matched array. Those of the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10} kept their sign too (<< F.g3b_provisional_dense_vs_grad_ib_delta_range >>, << F.g3b_provisional_dense_vs_mag_ib_delta_range >> and << F.g3b_provisional_matched_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB). With the room field projected out, $\Delta$ was unchanged in the scaled adults (<< F.g3b_scaled_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >>, against << F.g3b_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB). It was lower in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates (<< F.g3b_infant2yr_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta >>, against << F.g3b_infant2yr_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> dB). Section~\ref{sec:S10.2} gives the other heads' values.

The adult's placement spread (<< F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> dB) exceeded $\Delta$ in the school-age scaled adult (<< F.g3b_school_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> dB), the only principal smaller head with so small a $\Delta$. It also exceeded $\Delta$ in children A, B and C, whose own $D$ varied by << F.g3b_child_a_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >>, << F.g3b_child_b_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> and << F.g3b_child_c_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> dB over the family. Against each head's family median instead of top contact, the difference of pooled medians from the adult shrank by << F.g3b_school_family_vs_top_shrink >> and << F.g3b_size2yr_family_vs_top_shrink >> dB in the scaled adults and by << F.g3b_infant2yr_family_vs_top_shrink >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_family_vs_top_shrink >> dB in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates. It shrank by << F.g3b_infant18mo_family_vs_top_shrink >> dB in the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template and by << F.g3b_children_family_vs_top_shrink_range >> dB in the children.

The OPM arrays were refitted to each head by the adult's rules and kept in every helmet, with sensor noise, room field and analysis band unchanged. The smaller heads carry << F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> dense and << F.g3b_smaller_matched_sites_range >> site-matched sites, at a median standoff of << F.g3b_all_dense_standoff_2dp_range >> mm above each head's own MRI scalp. The background variance per unit cortical area was calibrated once on the adult, so a smaller cortex carries less total background; as a sensitivity analysis, it was multiplied by << F.g3b_cfg_bg_factor_low >> and by << F.g3b_cfg_bg_factor_high >>.

Fit and head size act on $D$ through at least four routes. A wider SQUID gap and a shallower cortex raise it, whereas fewer OPM sites and, in the school-aged children, a near-adult cortex close to the OPMs (unverified near-scalp anatomy; Sections~\ref{sec:S4.3} and \ref{sec:S10}) lower it. The constructions below combine these routes differently, and none isolates one (main text, Section 3.5).

\subsection{The helmet scaled with the head}\label{sec:S5.2}

Every coil center is scaled about the head origin by the child-to-adult ratio of head circumferences, keeping coil size, orientation and noise. The factor is raised in steps until no coil is within << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >> mm of the scalp; unlike the sphere's constant gap (Section~\ref{sec:S2}), the gaps scale with the head. The helmet was scaled about the centered head and, as a variant that removes the templates' lateral offset, about the laterally centered head (Section~\ref{sec:S8.2} records when the variant was added). The construction asks what the adult's fit would give at a child's size and is not a pediatric SQUID device. The main text treats it as secondary because it also brings the sensors closer than the adult's own fit (see below).

In its own scaled helmets, the adult's $D$ was << F.g3b_adult_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> and << F.g3b_adult_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB (centered and laterally centered; scale factor << F.g3b_adult_cfx_k >>). Scaled about the laterally centered head, the helmet left the OPM its absolute advantage ($D$ << F.g3b_principal_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads). $\Delta$ against the adult in its own scaled helmet, however, was negative in every principal smaller head. It was << F.g3b_school_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.g3b_school_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> and << F.g3b_size2yr_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.g3b_size2yr_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> dB in the scaled adults, and << F.g3b_infant2yr_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.g3b_infant2yr_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.g3b_infant12mo_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> dB in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates, whose intervals include zero. These parcel-bootstrap intervals exclude placement and construction uncertainty. With the room field projected out, the difference of medians from the adult was << F.g3b_principal_cfx_dense_vs_combined_proj_d_minus_adult_cfx_range >> dB in these heads (<< F.g3b_principal_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_cfx_range >> dB with sensor plus brain noise). In the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}, $D$ was << F.g3b_provisional_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB and $\Delta$ << F.g3b_provisional_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB. Only child C had a positive point estimate (<< F.g3b_child_c_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.g3b_child_c_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> dB), an interval that also contains its fixed-helmet $\Delta$; its scaled helmet keeps a median gap of << F.g3b_child_c_cfx_gap >> mm against the adult's << F.g3b_adult_cfx_gap >> mm.

Scaled about the centered head, the helmet left a residual gain in the off-center << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template (<< F.g3b_infant2yr_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> dB; $\Delta$ << F.g3b_principal_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB over the principal smaller heads). It did so too in the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template and child C (<< F.g3b_infant18mo_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> and << F.g3b_child_c_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> dB).

Three checks qualify the drop of $\Delta$ in the scaled helmet. First, part of the drop comes from the reference: against the adult at top contact, the difference of medians was << F.g3b_principal_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_top_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads (<< F.g3b_provisional_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_top_range >> dB in the others). Second, the gap scales with the helmet and shrank below the adult's << F.g3b_adult_cfx_gap >> mm in every principal smaller head (<< F.g3b_principal_cfx_gap_range >> mm) and in all the others but child C. This tighter fit than the adult's favors Neuromag, and the helmet fitted at the adult's gap avoids it (Section~\ref{sec:S5.3}). Third, the principal smaller heads carry fewer OPM sites (<< F.g3b_principal_dense_sites_range >> against << F.g3b_adult_dense_sites >>). The adult's array subsampled to those counts lost << F.g3b_principal_site_penalty_vs_combined_range >> dB, so at an equal OPM site count $\Delta$ in the fixed helmet would be << F.g3b_principal_eqcount_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB.

\subsection{The helmet fitted at the adult's gap}\label{sec:S5.3}

The Neuromag helmet is scaled about the head origin of each head's laterally centered pose until the median gap from magnetometer coil center to scalp equals the adult's. The target is << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >> mm, the adult's gap in its own helmet about its laterally centered head (scale factor << F.rev_cgap_adult_fitted_k >>), or, as a variant, << F.rev_cgap_adult_top_gap_mm >> mm, its gap at top contact in the fixed helmet. Coil size, orientation, integration points and noise are kept, and no coil center may come within the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing of the scalp or lie inside the head. In child C, this clearance binds first: the helmet stops at a gap of << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_mm >> mm, << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_above_target_mm >> mm above the target, with a scale factor of << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_k >> instead of << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_k_at_target >>.

Because the OPM arrays are those of the fixed helmet, the within-head contrast is a property of Neuromag alone, whichever OPM array is compared. The contrast is the area-weighted median over a head's targets of $F(v) = D_{\mathrm{fixed}}(v) - D_{\mathrm{fitted}}(v)$ (main text, Eq.~(3)), with the fixed helmet at top contact. Intervals use << F.rev_cgap_n_boot_primary >> parcel-bootstrap resamples for the dense array in the fixed helmet and in the helmet fitted at << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >> mm, and << F.rev_cgap_n_boot_secondary >> elsewhere. The fitted helmet (Table~\ref{tab:S5}) is a geometric control at the adult's median gap, not at its gap distribution, placement or coverage, and not a pediatric SQUID system.

