The within-head contrast is what Neuromag loses in a head to the fit, gap and placement of the fixed helmet together. It was << F.rev_cgap_principal_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads, every interval above zero, and << F.rev_cgap_provisional_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >> dB in the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}. The adult's was << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >> dB, because its laterally centered pose in its own helmet is looser than top contact. Part of any contrast is the construction itself. At an equal median gap, between top contact in the fixed helmet and the helmet fitted at its own top-contact gap, the adult's contrast was << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >> << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib_ci >> dB. Child C's negative contrast, with its two gaps nearly coinciding, is of this kind (Section~\ref{sec:S10.3}).

The interaction, the extra that the fixed helmet gives a smaller head beyond the adult, is the $\Delta$ estimator applied to $F$ instead of $D$ (Section~\ref{sec:S1}). It was << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_range >> dB in the scaled adults and << F.wr_cgap_principal_templates_interaction_range >> dB in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates, every interval above zero. In the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10} it was << F.wr_cgap_provisional_interaction_range >> dB. Table~\ref{tab:S6} gives the non-additivity residual per head.

For equal OPM site counts, the adult's dense array was subsampled by farthest-point sampling to each smaller head's dense site count (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> sites), the adult in the same fitted helmet. At the adult's gap, $\Delta$ was then << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads, every interval above zero. It was << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB in the scaled adults and << F.rev_cgap_infant2yr_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> and << F.rev_cgap_infant12mo_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> dB in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates. A smaller head's dense array thus gains a little at the adult's median gap and equal site count. This may be because its cortex lies closer to the OPMs, which these controls do not test. In the principal smaller heads, the fewer sites lower $\Delta$ by about as much. In the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}, $\Delta$ at equal site count was << F.rev_cgap_provisional_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB, and in children A and B the fewer sites lower it by more than this gain (Section~\ref{sec:S10.3}).

As a measure of placement uncertainty, $\Delta$ in the fixed helmet was computed, as a difference of medians, at each of the << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements (Table~\ref{tab:S7}). It was positive at every placement in each of the << F.wr_n_principal_words >> principal smaller heads, whose wider crossed bands also stayed above zero. In the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}, child C's band and every child's crossed band included zero (Section~\ref{sec:S10.2}). Top contact was not an extreme of any band.

